  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a strong recovery day at Port Washington post-Labor Day, but Hicksville remains structurally constrained. You need to see both stores clearly.\n\n**Port Washington is running hot.** Yesterday's $3,895 revenue (+31% vs last Wednesday) came on 7 active groomers at $509/groomer\u20148% above your trailing-30-day baseline of $472. The core groom revenue jumped 42%, a real productivity signal. However, retail cratered 48% while transactions dropped 10%, suggesting front-of-house focus (suggestive selling, point-of-sale execution) has softened. That's a fixable operational gap. Week-to-date is down 18%, but that's entirely Labor Day calendar effect\u2014the holiday landed Monday, suppressing mid-week bookings. MTD you're at $25.4K against a DOW-adjusted expectation of $29K, a real 12.5% shortfall, but year-to-date you're still crushing last year at +43%, so the store has momentum. One concern: your lapse pool of 505 customers represents $318K in annual value\u2014worth a reactivation push.\n\n**Hicksville is underpowered.** Yesterday you ran just 2 groomers at $490/groomer (20% above the baseline of $406, which is good)\u2014but that's the problem. You're scheduled for only 5 groomers today and tomorrow, and next 7 days shows 21 groomer-days (3/day average) versus Port Washington's 40. MTD Hicksville is flat revenue but *up* 13% in appointments\u2014customers are there, but you're leaving money on the table by not having capacity. Add-ons collapsed to 20% attach yesterday. This is a capacity-driven miss, not a performance issue. Retail is also down 54%, same pattern as Port Washington.\n\nFix: prioritize groomer hiring or scheduling at Hicksville; audit point-of-sale discipline at both locations on retail add-ons. Reactivate that 505-customer pool at Port Washington immediately.\n\n**Today: Port Washington 8 groomers (15h bathers), Hicksville 5 groomers (0h bathers); next 7 days Port Washington 40 groomer-days versus Hicksville 21.**"
